Data Engineer:


Structured data refers to data that has a defined data model and is organized in a clear and predictable manner. This type of data is typically found in relational databases, where it is organized into tables with rows and columns, each containing a specific type of information.

Semi-structured data, on the other hand, has some structure but does not conform to the strict schema of structured data. It may contain tags, markers, or other indicators to separate elements of the data, but there is more flexibility in how the data is organized and stored. Examples include XML, JSON, and YAML formats.

Unstructured data lacks a predefined data model and is typically stored in a raw or natural format, without any organization or structure. This type of data includes things like text documents, images, videos, audio files, and social media posts. Analyzing unstructured data often requires advanced techniques such as natural language processing or image recognition to extract meaningful insights.


